      "textEn": "271\n\nThe species are the same and ultimately merge into the atoms. In this world, nothing is permanent. Many kinds of things and their titles are our mental puzzles. The ultimate truth is beyond delusion, and it manifests in three forms—Nirgunbrahma, Arntayami Paramatma, and Purna Purushottam Bhagavan—. The ultimate realization of the infinite truth is only the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is called Vasudeva by devotees.\nThey say. Until the dust of the feet of devotees is worn on the forehead, no one\ncan become a devotee of God. Jad Bharat also told the king about his previous birth and said that by the grace of God,\nhe remembers all the events of his past life. Due to the deeds of his previous birth, the inert Bharat was very\ncareful. To avoid meeting worldly men, they adopted the behavior of a mute and deaf-mute person. The company of the three Gunas is very strong.\nThe only way to avoid the bad company of materialistic men is to associate with devotees. With their company, nine types of devotions can be performed—listening, chanting, remembering Vishnu, serving his feet, worshiping, bowing, serving, friendship, self-surrender. In this way,\nBy associating with devotees, by associating with material things, one can be freed from the ocean of ignorance,\nOne can attain the abode of Shri Bhagwan. Rahugan said,\nSalutations to the one who is the cause of the universe.\nTo the one who is considered insignificant. Salutations to the one who is the incarnation of the two-faced one.\nTo you, the mysterious, eternal, and experiential one. 1. Meaning of the word\n\nRahugana said—King Rahugan said; Namah—My salutations; Namah—Salutations; Karan-vigrahaya—Because of all causes,\nTo the one who manifests from the Supreme Being; Swaroop-tishchikit-vigrahaya—Removing the contradictions of the scriptures by revealing one's true form\nNamah—Salutations; Avadhuta—Oh Yogeshwar; Dwija-bandhu-linga—Through the characteristics of a man born in the Brahmin clan (even if\nOne who does not perform the actions of a Brahmin); Nigudh—concealed; Nitya-anubhava—that by which one attains the eternal self-realization; Tulyam—like. King Rahugana said—Oh great one, you are inseparable from the Supreme Personality of Godhead. By your\ninfluence, all the contradictions of the scriptures have been removed. You, in the guise of a brother of Brahma, are hiding your divine\nblissful form. I greet you with a respectful bow."
