      "textEn": "274\n\nIt is experiential, but there is no fatigue in reality. That is just for show. Thus,\nno conclusion can be drawn about the ultimate truth from such questions and answers. From your statement,\nMy mind is somewhat disturbed. Meaning: Formal questioning regarding the bodily-mind does not lead to the knowledge of the absolute truth. The formal understanding of physical pleasures and pains is different from the knowledge of the ultimate truth. In the Bhagavad Gita,\nShri Krishna tells Arjuna that the experience of happiness and sorrow through the body is momentary; they keep coming and going. A person should not be distracted by these, but rather, enduring them, remain engaged in attaining the self-essence.\nRemain. Brahman said,\nThis man, named, walks on the earth,\nWho is the earth, O Partha, for whose sake? Tasyapi chaṇṭharyoradhi guḷbajṇhā-\njānūrumadhyorśirodharaṁsaḥ. In the beginning, in the middle, and in the end, in the one who is like a porter,\nHe who is called the king of the hundred horses, he is present.\nIn whom is the pride of his own self,\nI am the king, like the ocean, foolish and blind. 6. Meaning of the word\n\nBrahmana said—Brahman said; Ayam—this; Jana—man; Naam—name; Chalan—while walking; Prithivyam—on the earth; Yah—who; Parthivah—of the earth; Parthivah—Oh king, whose body is of the earth; Kasya—of whom; Hetah—because; Tasyaapi—of him; Bhi—also; Angaharyooh—feet; Adhi—above; Gulphah—ankles; Jangahah—thighs; Janu—knees; Uru—thighs; Jagahah—leg; Jaanu—calves; Janu—knees; Mu—middle; Kati—waist; Waist; head; neck; shoulder; upper; arm; wooden;\nPalanquin; and; as; as on; Sauvira-king; thus; thus; well-known;\nHe is; in which; you; seated; own pride; false pretense; king; I am; in the kingdom of Sindhu; thus; thus; blind; arrogant; blinded by pride; The accomplished Brahmin Jada Bharat said—In many material combinations, there are various forms and earthly\ntransformations exist.\nFor some reason, these move on the earth and 